                  <h2>Critical Illness Insurance: A Useful Addition</h2>
                  <p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Critical_illness_insurance" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Critical illness insurance</a> pays a lump sum when a listed illness, such as cancer of specified severity, is diagnosed, regardless of the actual hospital bill. The money can go towards outpatient drugs, travel, home care or lost income. Most plans, however, have an initial waiting period, a survival period after diagnosis and standard definitions that may exclude very early cancers such as carcinoma in situ.</p>
<?= blog_image('does-health-insurance-cover-lung-cancer-treatment-2.webp', 'Calculator and hospital bills used to estimate lung cancer treatment costs', 'A written estimate of the full treatment pathway helps you plan beyond the insurance cover.') ?>

                  <h2>Government Schemes That Cover Lung Cancer Treatment</h2>
                  <ul>
                    <li><strong>Ayushman Bharat PM-JAY:</strong> the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayushman_Bharat_Pradhan_Mantri_Jan_Arogya_Yojana" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana</a> provides cover of up to ₹5 lakh per family per year at empanelled hospitals, with packages for cancer surgery, chemotherapy and radiotherapy. Since 2024, all citizens aged 70 and above are eligible regardless of income.</li>
                    <li><strong>Aarogyasri (Telangana):</strong> the state scheme covers eligible families for major treatments, including many cancer procedures, at empanelled hospitals.</li>
                    <li><strong>CGHS and ESI:</strong> central government employees, pensioners and insured workers can use these schemes for cancer care at recognized hospitals.</li>
                    <li><strong>Employer group policies:</strong> check your company cover, which often has no waiting period, and whether it can be topped up.</li>
                  </ul>

                  <h2>Costs Insurance May Not Cover</h2>
                  <ul>
                    <li>Outpatient consultations and follow-up scans, unless you have OPD cover</li>
                    <li>Some targeted therapy tablets taken at home, depending on the policy</li>
                    <li>Biomarker and genomic tests done on an outpatient basis</li>
                    <li>Travel, accommodation and food for family members</li>
                    <li>Nutritional supplements, home nursing and some equipment</li>
                  </ul>
                  <p>Planning for these costs early helps. Ask for a written estimate of the full treatment pathway, including how many <a href="/blog/how-many-rounds-of-chemo-for-stage-4-cancer">rounds of chemotherapy may be needed for advanced disease</a>, which <a href="/blog/which-chemotherapy-works-best-for-my-cancer-type">biomarker tests guide the choice of chemotherapy</a>, and whether <a href="/blog/do-i-need-chemotherapy-after-cancer-surgery">chemotherapy after cancer surgery</a> is likely. Many pharmaceutical companies also run patient assistance programs for expensive cancer drugs.</p>
<?= blog_image('does-health-insurance-cover-lung-cancer-treatment-3.webp', 'Indian rupee notes representing out-of-pocket cancer treatment expenses', 'Outpatient drugs, travel and home care are common out-of-pocket costs to budget for.') ?>

                  <h2>Tips to Avoid Claim Rejection</h2>
                  <ul>
                    <li>Disclose your full medical history and smoking status when buying a policy.</li>
                    <li>Renew on time, because a break in cover resets waiting periods.</li>
                    <li>Read the exclusions and sub-limits before admission, not after.</li>
                    <li>Start pre-authorization as early as possible.</li>
                    <li>Keep copies of every report, prescription and bill.</li>
                    <li>Ask for costs in writing, a habit that is part of the <a href="/blog/cancer-hospital-doctor-with-honest-guidance">honest guidance every cancer patient deserves</a>.</li>
                  </ul>
                  <p>If a claim is unfairly rejected, first use the insurer’s grievance process and then approach the Insurance Ombudsman. For questions about the surgery itself, see our <a href="/faqs">frequently asked questions about cancer surgery</a>.</p>

                  <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
                  <p>So, does health insurance cover lung cancer treatment? For most hospital-based care in India, yes: surgery, chemotherapy, radiotherapy and many modern treatments are covered, subject to your sum insured, waiting periods, sub-limits and exclusions. Read your policy carefully, disclose your history honestly, plan for outpatient costs and use government schemes if you are eligible. With the paperwork organized, you and your family can focus on lung cancer treatment and recovery.</p>
